    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for the USMLE and I'm a bit stuck on the pharmacology of ACE inhibitors. Can you explain the mechanism of action for Lisinopril, specifically how it interacts with the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) to lower blood pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, works by blocking the Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). This enzyme is crucial for converting Angiotensin I into Angiotensin II, which is a potent vasoconstrictor and a key driver of high blood pressure. By inhibiting ACE, Lisinopril prevents the formation of Angiotensin II, leading to vasodilation and a subsequent drop in blood pressure. It also reduces aldosterone secretion, which helps decrease sodium and water retention, further contributing to blood pressure reduction.",
      "source": "target",
